                <h2>Start With the Conclusion: Volume Is the Least Effective Approach</h2>
<p>The first two weeks after launching a new product on TikTok Shop are critical for training your ad delivery model. Most teams default to uploading 20-30 creatives simultaneously and letting the algorithm sort it out. This approach feels safe, but it's actually burning budget inefficiently.</p>
<p>The truly efficient cold start strategy uses fewer but more differentiated creatives to accelerate model learning. Specifically: <strong>10-15 highly differentiated creatives with a 3-5 day concentrated testing cycle</strong> typically outperforms 30 similar creatives.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Creative Diversity Matters More Than Volume</h2>
<p>What does "diversity" mean in practice? It means each creative should differ meaningfully in <strong>core elements</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Use scenarios</strong>: A home product shown in the kitchen vs. bedroom</li>
<li><strong>Persona</strong>: Real user in-frame vs. product close-up with captions</li>
<li><strong>Information pacing</strong>: Strong hook in 0-3 seconds vs. 10-second narrative arc</li>
<li><strong>Visual style</strong>: Raw camera footage vs. polished editing</li>
</ul>
<p>A utility app team launching in Southeast Asia prepared 25 creatives initially—all following the same "product feature showcase + discount" format. After three days with no standout performers, they realized the 25 creatives were essentially variations of a single creative logic.</p>
<p>They restructured the creative pool: kept 5 core product showcase videos, then added genuine user scenarios, comparison demonstrations, and mini-story content. Within one week, 3 creatives emerged with CTR significantly above their baseline.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Cold Start Creative Pool Checklist</h2>
<p>When building your creative pool from scratch, structure it this way:</p>
<p><strong>A. Product Showcase (4-5 creatives)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Core feature close-ups with key selling points in captions</li>
<li>Pain point intro + solution straight-shoot</li>
<li>Before/after comparisons</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>B. Scenario-Based Content (4-5 creatives)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Real user scenarios—Southeast Asian audiences prefer diverse contexts</li>
<li>Localized lifestyle integration beyond product display</li>
<li>UGC-style authentic sharing</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>C. Experimental Creatives (2-3 creatives)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Different hook formats: numeric, question-based, counterintuitive</li>
<li>Varying lengths: 15-second short version vs. 30-60 second full story</li>
<li>Different music styles or caption layouts</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>D. Creative Variations (2-3 creatives)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Different aspect ratios of the same footage</li>
<li>Same script with different voiceovers or subtitle languages</li>
<li>A/B versions of thumbnails and headlines</li>
</ul>
<p>This gives you a complete cold start pool of 12-16 creatives covering all major creative directions with clear differentiation between each one.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Don't Test Too Long—Or Too Short</h2>
<p>A 3-5 day concentrated testing period is an operational benchmark. Too short reveals no trend; too long burns unnecessary budget. During testing, use these benchmarks:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Creatives with CTR below 1%</strong> can typically be paused immediately</li>
<li><strong>Creatives with under 40% 5-second retention</strong> need hook adjustments</li>
<li><strong>Creatives generating conversions but at high cost</strong>: monitor whether CVR can be optimized</li>
</ul>
<p>After testing ends, preserve your top 2-3 performers as primary creatives while fine-tuning any "potential" creatives that showed promise.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Conclusion: Cold Start Needs Speed and Precision</h2>
<p>TikTok Shop's algorithm has a learning period for new accounts. During this phase, the biggest risk is <strong>feeding the system too many similar creatives, leaving the algorithm confused about what the target user actually looks like</strong>.</p>
<p>So the core principle for cold start isn't "how many creatives"—it's "do these creatives have enough diversity to let the algorithm learn quickly?"</p>
<p>Next time you're preparing for a new product cold start, ask yourself this: <strong>If I deleted half of these creatives, would the remaining ones still cover all core creative directions?</strong> If yes, your diversity is sufficient. If not, you still have too much homogenization and need to restructure.</p>
